# AUTHOR LINE GROUPING — the ONE-LINE-CONTAINER cell, and it is a NEGATIVE.
#
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture is a deliberately
# UNFORMATTED INPUT — the assertion is on what `gg fmt` turns it INTO
# (`fmt_author_rows_single_line_container_is_not_a_row`, exact text). A sweep
# would rewrite it into its own expected output and the cell would go
# green-vacuous.
#
# AXIS = grouping shape × container spans one line. The author wrote the whole
# container INCLUDING its delimiters on one line, with a magic trailing comma.
# The comma still says EXPLODE; there is no author newline anywhere, so there is
# nothing to say WHERE — and the ENGAGEMENT GUARD hands the list to the ratified
# one-element-per-line shape rather than inventing a single half-exploded row.
#
# The distinction from `single_row_multiline.gg` is the OPEN DELIMITER's line:
# there the author broke after `[`, here not at all. Both must reach the same
# output, and for the same reason — no break BETWEEN elements.
#
# NO PRE-FIX RED IS POSSIBLE (this is preserved behaviour, not added
# behaviour). RED-verified by BREAKING the mechanism: with the engagement guard
# removed, the list comes out as `[\n        1, 2, y,\n    ]` and the
# exact-text assertion reds. Restored after measuring.
from std.collections import Vector

void main():
    int y = 3
    Vector[int] xs = [1, 2, y,]
    print(xs.len())
